.SH A) OVERVIEW
shared code (SHARED, GLOBAL, and CONFIG verbs) are executed which requested
using the REUSE verb.  this allows useful actions and checks to be created
just once and executed a myriad.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
REUSE places a function call to the shared code in your script, nothing more.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
the REUSE verb is a single field, with a description, but that description
will be overwritten when koios updates/converts the file.  REUSE is a single,
un-indented line containing two (2) columns using ascii field-seperator (0x1c)
delimiters. these field delimiters will be shown here with the (§) character.

   verb-------- § ---desc-------------- §
   ···REUSE -a- § call initial setep    §    runs SHARED "a"
   ···REUSE -M- § call initial setep    §    runs GLOBAL "M"
   ···REUSE -ò- § call initial setep    §    runs CONFIG "ò"

.SH D) VERB
REUSE is the verb and must have three (3) character indentation.  also,
the share idenfitier must be suffixed with -#- where "#" is the identifing
character.  this field is a maximum of twelve (12) characters, overflow and
trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb-------- § ---desc-------------- §
   ···REUSE -a- § call initial setep    §    runs SHARED "a"

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
while you can add a description, it will be automatically overwritten by
the description of the share when koios source is updated/converted.

.SH F) FILTERING
reuse can pass specific filter requests, please refer to KOIOS_WHICH (5).

.SH G) OUTPUT
there is no specific output for the REUSE verb.  when it runs the share
header is shown and this can only happen if a REUSE exists.  nuff said.


